with japanese prime minister shinzo abe  set to resign  the future of japan's relations with  countries in the region  is suddenly an open question during his  tenure  abe sought to build an alliance with the  united states  india and australia primarily with the  aim of countering china's influence  i'll be speaking to an expert about what  the future of those ties is  but first here's more on the strategic  direction  abe gave japan  shinzo abe steered japan's defense  strategy  in a radical new direction he pledged to  revise japan's pacifist  post-world war ii constitution to  counter  changing security threats in the region  in his first speech to the nation he  said the security of japan is not  someone else's problem  it is a crisis that exists right there  and now  those words set the tone for japan's  defense over his years as prime minister  as abe sought to ward off a newly  assertive china  making incursions into japanese waters  and expanding its territorial claims in  the south china sea  north korea has also posed a  nuclear-armed menace  with frequent missile tests  to demonstrate his muscular stance abe  loosened a ban on  arms exports and in 2015 his party  pushed through  controversial legislation for so-called  collective  self-defense allowing japanese troops to  engage  in overseas combat with allied forces  the law drew strong opposition  domestically and a later push to revise  the pacifist constitution  failed  abe's military reforms and containment  diplomacy  have upset china its largest trading  partner  allies like u.s president donald trump  have confronted china with sanctions  but abe's had to find the balance  between competing and cooperating with  china  despite his radical security ambitions  a needle his successor will likely also  have to thread  so what happens next simon denyer is the  washington post bureau chief for japan  and the koreas  and he joins me now from tokyo simon  i'd like to look at japan's relations  with china first  where do you see them headed after  shinzo abe steps down  there's been quite a lot of ups and  downs in the relationship between japan  and china they started off  really badly under rabe's tenure he's  worked quite hard to improve them  actually in the last  few years so you know it's going to be  very interesting  to see whether china tries to exploit  this  uh and push its territorial claims to  the senkaku islands it's already  threatening  to do that it's been more active around  the senkaku diyou islands as  of course as china calls them um so  so we may see china as it's done with  india seeing an opportunity to push its  territorial claims  in the absence of our bay and while this  leadership transitions going on that's  something to keep an eye on  there was some talk of a so-called new  era in ties  between japan and china and it was  mostly down to shinzo abe he in fact  visited china as well to try and  smoothen things over  do you think that is a thing of the past  now  i mean i don't think it's necessarily a  thing of the past i think that whoever  comes in  after abe is likely to continue to try  to do this but you know  japan china relations they're never  going to be best of friends they do  have wartime issues between them japan  is an ally of america  china and the united states are  you know clearly not seeing eye to eye  far from it so  japan's kind of caught in the middle are  based on quite a good job i think of  patching things up and not letting that  sort of  not letting the china relationship  deteriorate  but i don't think you know we're really  talking about we're ever talking about  a golden era we're talking about sort of  both sides  putting their differences aside and just  getting on with things  more than more than real friendship  japan was also considered an  important partner for the united states  india and australia in the  so-called indo-pacific region do you see  that cooperation simon continuing  and broadly speaking yes i do i think  that you know japan is very committed to  being part of an alliance  of democracies in asia and beyond the  pacific region as you say  um it's going to be very interesting to  see how the relationship with the united  states develops though  if trump if president trump does get  another term in office i think that  japan probably would have liked to have  had  someone like shinzo abe who gets on very  well with trump  as their prime minister uh trump hasn't  really taken aim at japan but he's  always kind of threatened  to so it could be quite rocky for japan  if you don't have arbeit there and if  trump were  to win another term obviously if it's  biden  and whoever succeeds arbay i think  relations between japan and the us will  be pretty will be pretty smooth  lots of ifs and buts there but plenty to  watch out for but thanks so much  for this conversation simon daniel in  tokyo thank you so much  pleasure now there's a certainly tragic  irony and the fact that  the man who was elected to office eight  years ago primarily because of his  economic policies  will now leave the country as he himself  says in one of the worst economic crises  for decades  seven years eight months that's how long  shinzo abe was in office  longer than any other prime minister in  japan when he announced his resignation  citing health reasons  he focused on the crippling effects of  the pandemic on the japanese  economy this year's second quarter saw  economic growth shrink by almost  28 and how will things shape up without  rb the markets reacted to the news of  his resignation with pessimism  initial reports saw the nikkei index  dropped 2.1  by close of trading it was still 1.4  down  what's the mood on the streets of tokyo  i'm surprised he looked really tired but  i didn't think he would resign i wish he  could work harder and stay on in office  a bit longer  i've been wanting him to resign for a  long time  shinzo abe is a controversial figure in  japan partly because of his emphasis  on his public image in 2012 he  introduced his ambitious economic  programme  naming it unabashedly after himself  with ergonomics i will increase jobs and  incomes and revitalize the countryside  ubinomics was about easing monetary  policy the japanese central bank  cranked up the money press flooding the  market with fresh new  notes there were also credit financed  economic stimulus packages  initially the programme did increase  confidence both in the economy  and among the japanese population but  the risks of abanomics  soon became clear the program over  leveraged the economy to the point of  sovereign bankruptcy and led to a loss  of confidence on capital markets  the already struggling japanese economy  has been hit hard by the corona virus  with abe out of the picture it's up to  someone else to pull japan  out of the crisis  i spoke to professor jeff kingston  director of asian studies at temple  university japan in tokyo  and referring to shinzo abe's economic  strategy  i asked him what worked well and what  didn't  well the list of what did work well i  think is relatively  short um i think he was  good in terms of promoting free trade he  cut a deal with the  eu he also rescued the tpp  but on what didn't work he never  delivered  on structural reforms which were the  third  era of abenomics which are supposed to  improve  productivity he never delivered on  womenomics  he said he was going to help women shine  but  that hasn't happened there has been an  expansion of women's employment but it's  been in poorly paid dead-end careers in  non-regular employment  so that hasn't really worked out either  generally speaking  households and all sorts of polls say  they're not feeling the love  you know they didn't feel the trickle  down a lot of people dismiss avenomics  as welfare for the wealthy  corporations have done well hedge fund  managers have done well  but the average person doesn't see much  increase at all in their pay packet  and so the general sense of well-being  has not improved  all right and uh how big a role is or  has the pandemic been  playing in all this  um nomics was pretty much dead in the  water  you know at least two three years ago it  really was fading  so was already in trouble and then last  fall  he introduced an increase in the  consumption  tax which is regressive and that really  sent  consumption plummeting downward and  that's the real problem of the pandemic  is that people's income is shrinking a  lot of people are furloughed they're  losing their bonuses whatever  so there's been shrinking consumption  and  as people you know anticipate that the  economic typhoon  is going to be gathering force over the  coming months  people are battening down the hatches  spending less  and that creates sort of a downward uh  cycle  and so it doesn't look too bright so  problem for abe  is what's his legacy hobby nomics  certainly is a failure  he was hoping that the olympics might be  a feel-good send-off  but that doesn't look very likely and so  his pandemic response has generally  drawn  heavy criticism from the public they  don't think he's done very well so  he's tired out and that was professor  jeff kingston from temple university in  tokyo talking  to us earlier what time to bring in  chelsea delaney now  our financial market correspondent in  frankfurt chelsea good to see you  tell me what is the financial world  saying about shinzo abe's resignation  it has been a bit of a shock to the  markets and we've definitely seen that  reflected in  both the japanese stock market and also  the currency markets um  obviously there had been reports that  shinzo abe had been in and out of the  hospital  the past couple of weeks but nobody  really saw this coming today and  of course this does come at a terrible  time for japan when their economy is  facing  a record contraction shinzo abe  obviously a controversial figure but he  has been seen as really a stable hand  over the past several years so the loss  of that in this  really dire economic situation is a bit  of a blow but obviously the big question  now is is what's going to happen with uh  abenomics  and exactly and with 20 seconds to go  you have the chance to tell us who could  be the possible successor of  shinsu abe  there are a lot of names on on the  potential successor list it likely is  going to be somebody from his party the  liberal  the liberal democrats and that likely  will also  mean that abanomics is not gone maybe it  will have a new name but this  this policy of aggressive stimulus of  aggressive monetary policy is likely  going to stay in place with the economy  in this shape all right chelsea delaney  in frankfurt thank you  you

